= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Integrating the <probability density function> over the stated interval gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(T<8)=\int_0^8\frac1{25}e^{-t/25}\,dt
=1-e^{-8/25}=0.274\quad\text{(three significant figures)}.}
$$
The strict endpoint does not affect the <probability> for this <continuous probability distribution>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <survival function> of this <exponential distribution> is $\mathbb P(T>t)=e^{-t/25}$ for $t\ge0$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(T>20)=\int_{20}^\infty\frac1{25}e^{-t/25}\,dt
=e^{-20/25}=0.449\quad\text{(three significant figures)}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Subtract the two values of the <survival function>, or integrate the <probability density function> directly:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(14<T<30)
=\int_{14}^{30}\frac1{25}e^{-t/25}\,dt
=e^{-14/25}-e^{-30/25}
=0.270\quad\text{(three significant figures)}.}
$$
The final zero records the requested third significant figure.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <expectation> follows by <integration by parts>, with $u=t$ and $dv=e^{-t/25}\,dt/25$:
$$
\mathbb E[T]=\int_0^\infty t\,\frac1{25}e^{-t/25}\,dt
=\left[-te^{-t/25}\right]_0^\infty+\int_0^\infty e^{-t/25}\,dt.
$$
The endpoint term vanishes because the <exponential function> decays faster than $t$ grows. Hence the mean lifetime is
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[T]=25\ \text{months}.}
$$

